`classify_updates` in `tools/dependabot_merge.py` returns `unknown_metadata` for every signed Dependabot security update, because those commits leave `update-type` out of their `updated-dependencies` metadata. So even a security patch waits for a human.

Required: for a commit with exactly one dependency and no `update-type`, work out the update type from the commit subject (`bump NAME from A to B`, any prefix such as `chore(deps):` or `build(deps):`, `Bump` or `bump`, optional ` in /dir` suffix), but only when all of these hold:
- NAME equals the signed `dependency-name`;
- B equals the signed `dependency-version`;
- A and B are both plain dotted numbers (no prerelease or other suffix);
- B is newer than A.

Otherwise, and for any commit with more than one dependency, keep `unknown_metadata`. A derived type then goes through the existing policy unchanged: patch and minor updates are `compatible`, majors are `manual_update`, and a minor update below 1.0 is `prestable_update`.

The agent-dispatcher controller is changing how it retains model output. When no trusted secret scanner is configured it will omit the excerpt entirely (`"output_excerpt": null`) and say why, in new fields. `AgentAttempt` in `tools/ai_agent_schema.py` still expects `output_excerpt` to be a string, so ingestion rejects these privacy-safe records, and the new privacy fields would be dropped even if it accepted them. Controller error events (`"stage": "error"`) are rejected too.

The controller also needs a trusted hook to scrub an excerpt before keeping it, using the same verified Gitleaks scanner as ingestion. No such command exists.

Required:
1. `AgentAttempt` accepts and keeps (in `model_dump(by_alias=True)`) these optional fields, each `None` when absent:
   - `schema_version`: the integer `1`;
   - `controller_revision`: 40 lowercase hex characters, or `unknown`;
   - `excerpt_status`: one of `empty`, `omitted_no_scanner`, `omitted_scanner_failed`, `redacted`, `clean`;
   - `excerpt_truncated`: a boolean.
   `output_excerpt` may now be `None` (and is kept as `None`); a string excerpt keeps its 2,000-character limit. `error` is a valid `stage`. Records without the new fields are still accepted.
2. A new module `tools/ai_agent_excerpt.py`, run as `python -m tools.ai_agent_excerpt`, with:
   - `MAX_BYTES`, the stdin limit in bytes (16000);
   - `scrub(text)`: raises `OutputScanError` (from `tools.ai_output_scan`) when `scanner_path()` returns `None`. Otherwise it calls `scan_events` with the JSON text of `{"excerpt": text}` as its first positional argument and the scanner path, and returns the `excerpt` string from the cleaned JSON that `scan_events` returns. The module imports `scanner_path` and `scan_events` from `tools.ai_output_scan` and calls them by those names, so they can be replaced on the module.
   - `main()`: reads stdin (`sys.stdin`), passes it to `scrub`, and writes only the returned text to stdout, with nothing added. Input longer than `MAX_BYTES` bytes is rejected before it is scrubbed. On any failure (oversized or undecodable input, missing scanner, scan failure) it writes nothing to stdout and exits with `SystemExit` code 1, without diagnostics.

Run 35 opened a PR titled `docs: rEADME claims nightly drift`. `pr_title` in `agent_dispatcher/execute.py` lowercases the first character of the issue title so the subject passes the fleet's PR title rule (it must match `^(?![A-Z]).+$`), and that garbles acronyms and mixed-case names such as `README`, `CI`, `MiMo` or `X11`.

Required, for titles that aren't already Conventional (those stay unchanged):
1. When the subject's first word (up to the first space) has more than one character, the part after its first character isn't all lowercase (as `str.islower()` sees it, so `X11` counts), and the word is shorter than 36 characters, the word is wrapped in backticks instead of being lowercased, with any backtick inside it replaced by `'`. The rest of the subject is unchanged. Examples:
   - `README claims nightly drift` with the `documentation` label gives "docs: `README` claims nightly drift";
   - `MiMo times out` (`bug`) gives "fix: `MiMo` times out";
   - `CI` (no label) gives "chore: `CI`";
   - `X11 forwarding fails` (`bug`) gives "fix: `X11` forwarding fails";
   - "Read`ME is stale" gives "chore: `Read'ME` is stale".
2. Otherwise the first character is lowercased as today: `A flaky test` (`bug`) gives `fix: a flaky test`, `Add previews` (`enhancement`) gives `feat: add previews`, and a first word of 36 characters or more is lowercased too.
3. The title is still cut to 72 characters and right-stripped. The subject always passes the rule above and has an even number of backticks.
